ctivity/first-step {:db/id 17592186097413} :activity/id #uuid "64f2826e-10ec-5427-8fd0-5dacd740fef4" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186048749 :topic/id #uuid "bad8d582-b827-501b-8a06-58b93e5ecdb0" :topic/math-academy-id 1114} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186097413 :step/content {:db/id 17592186097813 :tutorial/content "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same. \n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." :tutorial/id #uuid "a71b1a8d-93f4-5e48-ab3a-bd7220d50f18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2611 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9f76f2b2-574f-5f45-a54b-c55d5040cccd" :step/math-academy-id 12306 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097414}} {:db/id 17592186097414 :step/content {:db/id 17592186096610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186096410 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186480049} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186097160} {:db/id 17592186097316} {:db/id 17592186237849} {:db/id 17592186237850} {:db/id 17592186314904} {:db/id 17592186314906} {:db/id 17592186338886} {:db/id 17592186480008} {:db/id 17592186480009} {:db/id 17592186480011} {:db/id 17592186480014} {:db/id 17592186480016} {:db/id 17592186480017} {:db/id 17592186480023} {:db/id 17592186480027} {:db/id 17592186480034} {:db/id 17592186480041} {:db/id 17592186480044} {:db/id 17592186480048} {:db/id 17592186524982} {:db/id 17592186525017}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "49950076-5f24-57dd-96a0-b8b061b4f2cb" :step/math-academy-id 12307 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097415}} {:db/id 17592186097415 :step/content {:db/id 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186096411 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186480109} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186096989} {:db/id 17592186096990} {:db/id 17592186235457} {:db/id 17592186235471} {:db/id 17592186272085} {:db/id 17592186272086} {:db/id 17592186284688} {:db/id 17592186284689} {:db/id 17592186297289} {:db/id 17592186480052} {:db/id 17592186480057} {:db/id 17592186480059} {:db/id 17592186480063} {:db/id 17592186480079} {:db/id 17592186480081} {:db/id 17592186480083} {:db/id 17592186480088} {:db/id 17592186480089} {:db/id 17592186480091} {:db/id 17592186480092} {:db/id 17592186480093} {:db/id 17592186480107} {:db/id 17592186525040}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "ef920bbd-db8e-5c63-82fa-370c8e9f05e9" :step/math-academy-id 12308 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097416}} {:db/id 17592186097416 :step/content {:db/id 17592186097814 :tutorial/content "If we replace  the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "061a5c54-700a-5793-b266-cf156584544a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2612 :tutorial/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"} :step/id #uuid "ef1a628c-101c-57a3-a8d3-5ce0731832a5" :step/math-academy-id 12309 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097417}} {:db/id 17592186097417 :step/content {:db/id 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14e68965-6c39-5947-ac05-eee99e685063" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186479997} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186096991} {:db/id 17592186097155} {:db/id 17592186253334} {:db/id 17592186284697} {:db/id 17592186297290} {:db/id 17592186314919} {:db/id 17592186314920} {:db/id 17592186479956} {:db/id 17592186479960} {:db/id 17592186479964} {:db/id 17592186479971} {:db/id 17592186479973} {:db/id 17592186479974} {:db/id 17592186479976} {:db/id 17592186479981} {:db/id 17592186479982} {:db/id 17592186479989} {:db/id 17592186479990} {:db/id 17592186479993} {:db/id 17592186479996} {:db/id 17592186524984} {:db/id 17592186543477} {:db/id 17592186543497} {:db/id 17592186543507}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"} :step/id #uuid "d2eb3d3f-2d57-59c0-b5a0-4253946c9cf7" :step/math-academy-id 12310 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097418}} {:db/id 17592186097418 :step/content {:db/id 17592186097815 :tutorial/content "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b006e01-04f6-5f6b-b435-b0f89163d7a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2613 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "be9c264f-1e13-55c5-83f7-3217ce14b437" :step/math-academy-id 12311 :step/next {:db/id 17592186097749}} {:db/id 17592186097749 :step/content {:db/id 17592186097806 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the rule\n![](images/77/77334bed6aafc3383552d90bcfc8da614d56493edce4e7ab89a2f28e01d3dbfa.svg)\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of ![](images/5b/5ba79af546498192229d0cdd5a4a922d1d015802402b8eec5910fe00f999820d.svg) \n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n![](images/09/091c69568c8472aacf8229a756a835e0edc26700677e4fa67de3d2f6ac0d96a2.svg)\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n![](images/c9/c9b146d550ef068823a4ee4b9f63eea89f46d6ceecedf0ad4baa6462f11d9127.svg)\n\nNow, the following result can be used (we'll prove it in the subsequent topics):\n\n![](images/ce/cedfc7ab127ac8b34cd72b21e8c3e527541e054721324d9d948d6df123cd61e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/5880fb665abda7f3cef4ad7425c99c43b16e84c448c7b9e22ff32f76e36df87c.svg)\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "551a7005-80c0-5cee-b28e-f3b565aaf250" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13257 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Second Rule"} :step/id #uuid "91e627df-d8f4-581a-bf44-76bf1bb88674" :step/math-academy-id 461037}] :activity/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186096892} {:db/id 17592186097413 :step/content {:db/id 17592186097813 :tutorial/content "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its 